node_modules/
dist/
.wrangler/
# Materialised copy of catalog-platform/packages/estate-auth (the canonical
# auth module). A build artifact of scripts/sync-estate-auth.mjs — never
# committed, because two copies of an auth file drift (estate-auth-design §1.1).
apps/worker/src/estate-auth/
# Materialised copy of catalog-platform's <estate-search> custom element (the
# canonical cross-catalog search box). A build artifact of
# scripts/sync-estate-search.mjs — never committed, so a search improvement made
# upstream reaches every site instead of dying in a per-repo fork.
apps/web/public/estate/
# Materialised copy of catalog-platform's estate THEME asset (estate-theme.css,
# theme.js, the self-hosted faces + their OFL text). A build artifact of
# scripts/sync-estate-theme.mjs — never committed, because the manual copy this
# replaces silently offered four themes for the whole day after `hearts`
# shipped upstream (2026-08-16). The whole directory is estate output; nothing
# hand-written has ever lived here. public/fonts/ is a DIFFERENT, older, still
# hand-maintained directory — do not confuse them.
apps/web/public/assets/
*.local
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.dev.vars
.DS_Store
*.log
# ⚠️ …EXCEPT the deploy record. `*.log` above would swallow it, and this file is
# the shared state two concurrent runs read to avoid deploying over each other —
# see scripts/deploy-guard.mjs. In `library_catalog` the same `*.log` line ate
# the same file and was caught only because someone noticed the commit had no
# log line in it; here the exception is written before the first line exists.
!docs/deploys.log
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json

# ⚠️ The deploy coordination lock — see scripts/deploy-guard.mjs. Local by
# design: it says "a deploy is running on THIS machine right now", which is not
# a fact any other checkout should inherit. The shared record is
# docs/deploys.log, which IS tracked.
.deploy.lock

# credentials-catalog F-3 (2026-08-17): cover .dev.vars variants + .env; repo is PUBLIC.
.env
.env.*
.dev.vars.*

# ⚠️ …EXCEPT the TEMPLATES, which carry names and no values.
#
# 🔴 **Last matching pattern wins, and these have to sit AFTER the block above.**
# There is already a `!.env.example` at line 24 and it does nothing: the
# `.env.*` on line 45 is later, so it wins, and `git check-ignore -v
# .env.example` names line 45 as the reason. 2026-08 audit, finding 23 — which
# blamed a `.env.*` at line 33 that has since moved, so the negation was never
# in the wrong place, the second copy of the pattern was added below it.
#
# ⚠️ Measured 2026-09-06: BOTH templates were ignored, and neither is tracked —
# so a fresh clone of this repo has no setup template at all, which is worse
# than the audit's version of the finding. Un-ignoring them is all this can do;
# committing them is a person's call, because it publishes a file to a PUBLIC
# repo. Names only, never values — check before adding either.
!.env.example
!.dev.vars.example

# Agent worktrees. ⚠️ This rule was LOCAL-ONLY in .git/info/exclude until
# 2026-08-21 — which is not shared, so a fresh clone on another machine had no
# protection at all. It matters because these worktrees hold
# apps/worker/.wrangler/tmp/*.js.map, and a wrangler dev source map INLINES the
# .dev.vars values: a live ANTHROPIC_API_KEY sitting in a build artefact nobody
# would think to look in, in a PUBLIC repo.
.claude/worktrees/
